[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate · 0:10] Great, let me play this back to make sure I've got it. Brew & Bean, 200 US locations, specialty coffee, revenue up 15% over two years, but net margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's an 18-point compression, which is enormous. The key insight right out of the gate is that this is not a demand problem. Revenue is growing, so whatever's happening is on the cost side or in the mix of what they're selling, and I'd bet it's structural rather than cyclical given it's sustained over two full years.

Here's how I'd structure it. First, I'd decompose profit into revenue and cost, and on the revenue side split into price times volume, then look at mix — specialty beverages versus drip coffee versus food attach. My hypothesis is that if same-store traffic is up around 12% and average ticket is roughly flat, they're essentially buying growth, which means they're discounting into volume.

Second, costs. I'd split fixed versus variable and go after COGS, labor, occupancy, and corporate overhead. In specialty coffee, green coffee prices, dairy, and wage inflation are the usual suspects, and all three have moved hard recently.

Third, footprint. Two years of growth at 200 locations suggests they've been opening stores, and new stores drag margin for 12 to 18 months.

Do we have a cost breakdown as a percentage of revenue for both years? And separately, do we have menu-price history — have they actually raised prices at all?
