[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:22] Understood. What's your recommendation to the client?

[candidate · 1:32] My recommendation is that Brew & Bean must take price immediately — a staged increase of roughly 12 to 15 percent across the menu over three quarters — because they have absorbed two years of 38 percent input inflation with zero pass-through, and no amount of procurement or operational work closes an 18-point gap.

The supporting logic. First, pricing is the only lever large enough. Labor is flat at 22 percent and already lean; overhead moved only 2 points. The entire problem sits in COGS, and inflation is broad-based across every input, so there's no supplier to renegotiate your way out of. Second, specialty coffee demand is famously inelastic — if traffic is up around 12 percent on flat pricing, they have demonstrable pricing power they simply haven't exercised. I'd expect to retain 90 percent of volume on a 12 percent increase. Third, run the math on the pass-through: at 58 percent COGS, a 15 percent price increase drops roughly 15 points straight to the bottom line, because COGS as a percentage of COGS doesn't move — so 15 points of price restores us to roughly 21 percent margin, essentially back to the historical 24.

Risks: competitive response if peers hold price, and brand perception in value-sensitive markets. I'd mitigate by leading with premium SKUs and holding the entry-level drip price.

Next steps: price elasticity testing in two regional markets over six weeks, a bean hedging program for the 25 percent exposure, and a waste audit on dairy.
