[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:45] Understood. Beyond what we’ve discussed, what else could the client do?

[candidate · 1:57] Plenty of levers beyond price. Let me go through them by bucket.

On COGS, the obvious one is portfolio reformulation — shifting mix toward higher-margin products. Drip coffee runs dramatically better margin than handcrafted espresso drinks loaded with dairy. If dairy and the non-bean basket is 75 percent of COGS, that's 75 points of revenue exposure sitting in milk, syrup, packaging and food, so every percentage point of mix we shift away from dairy-heavy drinks is worth real money. A five-point mix shift against a 75-point base is close to four points of margin recovery without touching price at all.

Second, supply chain. Even with broad-based inflation, a 200-unit chain has scale it may not be using — central roasting, direct trade contracts, forward purchasing twelve to eighteen months out, and consolidating distributors.

Third, waste. Dairy spoilage and end-of-day pastry write-offs typically run three to five percent of COGS in this format. That's recoverable with better forecasting and par-level discipline.

Fourth, labor productivity. Labor's flat at 22 percent, but flat isn't optimized — mobile order-ahead, better equipment, and simplified recipes raise throughput per labor hour.

Fifth, format. Smaller footprints, kiosks, drive-thru conversions lower occupancy per transaction.

Sixth, new revenue: packaged beans at retail, subscriptions, catering. High margin, leverages existing brand.

I'd sequence waste and mix first — fastest payback, no customer risk.
